.TH "restoreCheck" "1" "" "SequoiaDB User Manuals" ""
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
restoreCheck \- check a time for restore
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]db.restoreCheck([options])\f[]
.SH CATEGORY
.PP
Sdb
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The SequoiaDB cluster will calculate the consistency time window for global
recovery based on the current logs of each data node and the available log
space.
This function is used to check whether the specified time point is within the
current time window, or check whether the cluster can be restored to the
specified time point.
At the same time, it can be used to obtain the latest consistent point in time.
.SH PARAMETERS
.PP
options ( \f[I]object, optional\f[] )
.PP
Set the time point to check, the available options are as follows.
.IP \[bu] 2
Time ( \f[I]number/string/Timestamp\f[] ): Specify the target time point to be
checked, in seconds.
.RS 2
.PP
When the value of this parameter is 0, the latest consistent time point will be
checked.
When the value is a string, the value filled in should conform to the ISO 8601
format.
.PP
Format: \f[C]Time:0\f[] or \f[C]Time:1609430400\f[] or
\f[C]Time:"2021\-01\-01T00:00:00+08:00"\f[] or
\f[C]Time:Timestamp("2021\-01\-01T00:00:00+08:00")\f[]
.RE
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[]
.PP
On Unix\-based machines use \f[C]date\f[] to get or format timestamps.
.IP \[bu] 2
Get the current time in ISO 8601 time.
.RS 2
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ date \-Iseconds
2021\-01\-01T00:00:00+08:00
\f[]
.fi
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
Format date to ISO 8601 format.
.RE
.RS
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
```lang\-bash
$ date \-Iseconds \-\-date=\[aq]2021/01/01 15:11:09\[aq]
2021\-01\-01T15:11:09+08:00
```
\f[]
.fi
.IP \[bu] 2
Get the current timestamp.
.RS 2
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
$ date +%s
1609430400
\f[]
.fi
.RE
.RE
